WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:02.302
-You've been here a fair amount
throughout the Trump presidency.

00:00:02.302 --> 00:00:03.303 line:92%
We're now coming up on a year.

00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:04.804 align:start size:94% position:6%
Are you --
It's felt longer than a year,

00:00:04.804 --> 00:00:07.907
but based on where we are now
and where we were a year ago,

00:00:07.907 --> 00:00:08.875 line:92%
is this what you expected,

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:10.710 size:84% position:42%
and is there anything
that you didn't expect?

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:15.315
-Every day that has happened in
the last year, I didn't expect.

00:00:15.315 --> 00:00:17.417 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:17.417 --> 00:00:18.718 size:78% position:39% line:92%
I think I expected --

00:00:18.718 --> 00:00:21.287 align:start size:94% position:6%
In your monologue
or "A Closer Look,"

00:00:21.287 --> 00:00:25.392 size:75% position:38%
you talked about the
55 minutes of Trump

00:00:25.392 --> 00:00:27.160 align:start size:94% position:6%
negotiating immigration.
-Yeah.

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:30.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
-However, obviously not
well-versed on every issue,

00:00:30.230 --> 00:00:35.101 align:start size:94% position:6%
but in there --
and, you know, breathing.

00:00:35.101 --> 00:00:36.736 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah. Talking words.
Yeah.

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:37.737 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:37.737 --> 00:00:39.339 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I think
I expected more of that...

00:00:39.339 --> 00:00:41.975 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Uh-huh.
-...and less of the

00:00:41.975 --> 00:00:46.312
just bedlam and chaos
that has really marked the year.

00:00:46.312 --> 00:00:49.582 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We talked -- You mentioned
the Michael Wolff book

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:51.618 align:start size:94% position:6%
and the anger
that's coming out

00:00:51.618 --> 00:00:54.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
of the Trump White House
about it.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:55.121 line:92%
You think, to some degree,

00:00:55.121 --> 00:00:57.290
that speaks to the vanity
of the Trump White House.

00:00:57.290 --> 00:00:59.359 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah.
I mean, I think Michael Wolff

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:02.962
very clearly -- and I think
he's been pretty open about it.

00:01:02.962 --> 00:01:06.566 align:start size:94% position:6%
Early on, he wrote
a vaguely flattering piece

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:07.467 size:81% position:41% line:92%
about President Trump.

00:01:07.467 --> 00:01:12.105 align:start size:94% position:6%
More than it being flattering,
it had a nice picture...

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:12.505 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:14.774 align:start size:94% position:6%
-...on the cover
of "The Hollywood Reporter."

00:01:14.774 --> 00:01:15.508 size:84% position:42% line:92%
-It was a nice picture.

00:01:15.508 --> 00:01:17.677
-It was a very nice picture and
he apparently really liked it.

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:20.814 size:75% position:38%
-Yeah. He likes ones
with more pictures.

00:01:20.814 --> 00:01:21.881 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:21.881 --> 00:01:25.485
-And then he did an early
interview with Steve Bannon

00:01:25.485 --> 00:01:29.556 size:75% position:38% line:92%
that was offensively

00:01:29.556 --> 00:01:31.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
just handing him
the microphone and talking

00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:33.793
at a time when I think a lot
of people were really worried

00:01:33.793 --> 00:01:36.162
about Steve Bannon and what
ideas he was gonna bring in.

00:01:36.162 --> 00:01:39.766
But it was all what's called,
in my business, beat-sweetening.

00:01:39.766 --> 00:01:40.567 line:92%
He was sweetening the beat.

00:01:40.567 --> 00:01:42.936 align:start size:94% position:6%
He was gaining their trust,
and they fell for it.

00:01:42.936 --> 00:01:46.439
And, look, this is a guy who
has a reputation for doing this

00:01:46.439 --> 00:01:49.976
and making a lot of money and
being very successful doing it,

00:01:49.976 --> 00:01:52.312 line:92%
flattering his way into a story,

00:01:52.312 --> 00:01:54.948 size:75% position:38%
doing his particular
kind of journalism,

00:01:54.948 --> 00:01:59.519 align:start size:94% position:6%
and leaving a lot
of crying people behind him.

00:01:59.519 --> 00:02:02.322 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You know,
Bannon gets a lot of credit

00:02:02.322 --> 00:02:04.958 align:start size:94% position:6%
for being sort of
a crazy genius

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:05.992 line:92%
who figured out all these things

00:02:05.992 --> 00:02:07.794 align:start size:94% position:6%
and yet makes
a mistake like this.

00:02:07.794 --> 00:02:08.795 size:81% position:41% line:92%
Is it just more likely

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:12.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
that there was not
a lot of genius going on?

00:02:12.232 --> 00:02:13.600 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:13.600 --> 00:02:16.136
-Well, I don't think I've ever
called him a creative genius.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:19.272 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-But I think that --

00:02:19.272 --> 00:02:21.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
I think that,
first of all, where --

00:02:21.341 --> 00:02:24.978
Look, I wasn't there for a lot
of it, or for any of it, really.

00:02:24.978 --> 00:02:27.480 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I think
that certain journalists,

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:30.783
when they say something's off
the record, they really mean it.

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:33.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
They think it's --
You know, you can honor it.

00:02:33.253 --> 00:02:37.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
And certain journalists, it
doesn't mean anything to them.

00:02:37.023 --> 00:02:38.858 align:start size:94% position:6%
And they're happy
to burn sources

00:02:38.858 --> 00:02:40.360 line:92%
and run away from the story.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.896 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, and run away
after the story is done,

00:02:42.896 --> 00:02:45.865 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it seems as though
he did that a bunch of times.

00:02:45.865 --> 00:02:48.234 size:81% position:41% line:92%
So, I mean, I don't --

00:02:48.234 --> 00:02:49.669 line:92%
You know, Bannon's not denying

00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:51.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he said
these things in the book.

00:02:51.971 --> 00:02:53.540 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I can't believe
he would've said them

00:02:53.540 --> 00:02:55.008 align:start size:94% position:6%
thinking they were
gonna be in the book.

00:02:55.008 --> 00:02:56.976 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right.
It is pretty crazy.

00:02:56.976 --> 00:02:59.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
And the interesting thing is,
there is a parallel,

00:02:59.179 --> 00:03:00.446 align:start size:94% position:6%
because I feel
as though Donald Trump

00:03:00.446 --> 00:03:03.249
sort of burned a lot of people
on his way to the White House,

00:03:03.249 --> 00:03:05.251 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then, I think
he would justify it by saying,

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:06.052 line:92%
"I got to the White House,"

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.120 align:start size:94% position:6%
whereas Michael Wolff
did the same,

00:03:07.120 --> 00:03:08.588 align:start size:94% position:6%
and I think
he would justify it by being,

00:03:08.588 --> 00:03:10.823 align:start size:94% position:6%
"I'm the number-one book
on Amazon."

00:03:10.823 --> 00:03:12.392 align:start size:94% position:6%
-There is
a poetic justice to it...

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:16.229
-Yeah.
-..that this is the book so far

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.064 align:start size:94% position:6%
that defines
the Trump presidency

00:03:18.064 --> 00:03:18.731 size:88% position:44% line:92%
with somebody who has --

00:03:18.731 --> 00:03:22.535 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah, who has just
upended all standards.

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:25.238 align:start size:94% position:6%
And yet,
we in the media, journalists,

00:03:25.238 --> 00:03:27.407 align:start size:94% position:6%
are supposed
to uphold standards.

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:28.341 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-We're not supposed to

00:03:28.341 --> 00:03:31.277
lower them just according to --
I mean, you know.

00:03:31.277 --> 00:03:34.914 align:start size:94% position:6%
If I responded to --
This is the world we're in.

00:03:34.914 --> 00:03:36.482 align:start size:94% position:6%
If I responded
to President Trump

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:39.686 align:start size:94% position:6%
the way that
he writes about journalists,

00:03:39.686 --> 00:03:41.621 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, calling me
a flunky or whatever,

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:44.958
you would be like, "Oh,
I can't believe Jake did that."

00:03:44.958 --> 00:03:45.391 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
-Yeah.

00:03:45.391 --> 00:03:46.859 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You would --
You would be disappointed.

00:03:46.859 --> 00:03:47.927 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm glad you
hold the standard up.

00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:50.063 align:start size:94% position:6%
-You'd be like, "Well,
I wish he hadn't done that.

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:53.032 size:78% position:39%
I wish he hadn't --"
The President did it!

00:03:53.032 --> 00:03:54.968 align:start size:94% position:6%
The President --
The President did it.

00:03:54.968 --> 00:03:57.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we are expected
to uphold these standards.

00:03:57.136 --> 00:03:59.239 line:92%
So, I mean, that's why I'm not,

00:03:59.239 --> 00:04:02.475 align:start size:94% position:6%
you know, reading the book
on-air as gospel.

00:04:02.475 --> 00:04:04.711 align:start size:94% position:6%
We have
to be skeptical about it.

00:04:04.711 --> 00:04:06.779 align:start size:94% position:6%
-The real standard
in your family --

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:09.349 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Oh, yeah.
-No offense to you.

00:04:09.349 --> 00:04:11.618 line:92%
Your daughter, last October,

00:04:11.618 --> 00:04:14.087 size:88% position:44%
got an op-ed published
in the "New York Times."

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.488 align:start size:94% position:6%
-She's 10.
-She's 10.

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:16.589 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:04:16.589 --> 00:04:18.124 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, this is true
that she got an op-ed,

00:04:18.124 --> 00:04:20.893 align:start size:94% position:6%
and it's called --
by the way, she did not hide

00:04:20.893 --> 00:04:24.130 align:start size:94% position:6%
that she was 10.
The op-ed title is "I'm 10."

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:24.664 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.664 --> 00:04:26.766 align:start size:94% position:6%
"And I Want Girls
to Raise Their Hand."

00:04:26.766 --> 00:04:28.401 line:92%
And this is a true story.

00:04:28.401 --> 00:04:31.204 size:84% position:42% line:92%
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:31.204 --> 00:04:35.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
Your daughter came up
with her own Girl Scout badge.

00:04:35.708 --> 00:04:36.676 size:78% position:39% line:92%
-My kids are amazing,

00:04:36.676 --> 00:04:38.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
Jack, who's 8,
and Alice, who's 10.

00:04:38.444 --> 00:04:42.215 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Alice came home
from school and said,

00:04:42.215 --> 00:04:44.017 align:start size:94% position:6%
"The girls
don't raise their hands.

00:04:44.017 --> 00:04:44.951 line:92%
The boys raise their hands."

00:04:44.951 --> 00:04:47.487 size:84% position:42%
And she talked about it
with my wife, her mom.

00:04:47.487 --> 00:04:50.356 align:start size:94% position:6%
And she talked about it
with her Girl Scout troop,

00:04:50.356 --> 00:04:51.357 line:92%
and they came up with this badge

00:04:51.357 --> 00:04:53.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
to encourage girls
to raise their hands.

00:04:53.459 --> 00:04:54.560 line:92%
You have to pledge to do it

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.828 align:start size:94% position:6%
and get three other girls
to do it.

00:04:55.828 --> 00:04:58.631
She came up with the badge,
and when the badge came out,

00:04:58.631 --> 00:05:00.466 align:start size:94% position:6%
I was very proud,
and I put it out there.

00:05:00.466 --> 00:05:03.036
It was also right in the middle
of the #MeToo movement,

00:05:03.036 --> 00:05:04.404 line:92%
and it seemed like it's great.

00:05:04.404 --> 00:05:06.639 align:start size:94% position:6%
Because she wasn't blaming
anybody for anything.

00:05:06.639 --> 00:05:08.941
She was saying, "We need
to be in charge of this."

00:05:08.941 --> 00:05:11.511 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yep.
-And then, I tweeted it,

00:05:11.511 --> 00:05:12.612 align:start size:94% position:6%
and somebody
from the "New York Times,"

00:05:12.612 --> 00:05:14.180
a nice woman named
Bari Weiss reached out and said,

00:05:14.180 --> 00:05:16.349
"Would your daughter want
to write an op-ed for us,"

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:18.351 align:start size:94% position:6%
and she did it.
And honestly, these --

00:05:18.351 --> 00:05:20.386 align:start size:94% position:6%
Like, she's like,
"Oh, that's cool."

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:21.521 align:start size:94% position:6%
She doesn't know.
-Yeah.

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:23.389 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
Have you ever been asked

00:05:23.389 --> 00:05:24.724 align:start size:94% position:6%
to write an op-ed
in "The New York Times"?

00:05:24.724 --> 00:05:27.226 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Um, I've had a few in there,
but never --

00:05:27.226 --> 00:05:28.661 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:28.661 --> 00:05:30.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
It's more like
I've begged to get them in.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.232 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-I've never been solicited.

00:05:33.232 --> 00:05:35.168 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Right, right.
-Actually, that's not true.

00:05:35.168 --> 00:05:36.502 align:start size:94% position:6%
They asked me
to write something,

00:05:36.502 --> 00:05:37.970 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then they killed it.
-Really?

00:05:37.970 --> 00:05:39.405 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:40.707 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Did she ever go over
and be like,

00:05:40.707 --> 00:05:41.507 line:92%
"Hey, Dad, that's all right.

00:05:41.507 --> 00:05:44.043 align:start size:94% position:6%
Sometimes you win,
sometimes you lose"?

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:44.644 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:05:44.644 --> 00:05:46.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
Well, you have
an incredible family.

00:05:46.245 --> 00:05:47.613 align:start size:94% position:6%
I'm always happy to see them,
as well.

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:50.416 align:start size:94% position:6%
And thank you so much
for being here.

